== 6. Lost Joy

#strong[The sixth device that Satan has to keep a poor soul in a sad, doubting, and questioning condition, is,]

BY suggesting to the soul that surely his state is not good, because he cannot rejoice and delight in Christ as once he could, because he has lost the comfort and joy that once was in his spirit. Says Satan, “You know there was a time when your heart was much carried out in rejoicing and delighting in Christ. You do not forget the time when your heart used to be full of joy and comfort. But now, how you have fallen in your joys and comforts! Therefore your state is not good. You only deceive yourself to think that it was ever good; for surely if it had been, your joy and comfort would have continued.” And at this the soul is apt to take Satan’s part and say, “It is just so. I see all is worthless, and I have only deceived my own soul,” #emph[etc.]

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that the loss of comfort is a separable adjunct from grace; the soul may be full of holy affections when it is empty of divine consolations. There may be, and often#footnote[Ps 63:1, 2, 8. Isa 50:10. Mic 7:8, 9. Ps 42:5.] is, true grace, indeed much grace, where there is not a drop of comfort or a dram of joy. Comfort belongs not to the being but to the well-being of a Christian. God has not linked these two choice lovers together so closely that they cannot be#footnote[Spiritual joy is a sun that is often clouded; though it is as precious a flower as most that Paradise affords, yet it is subject to fade and wither.] parted. That wisdom that is from above will never work a man to reason this way: “I have no comfort, therefore I have no grace; I have lost the joy I once had, therefore my condition is not good, and was never good,” #emph[etc.] But it will enable a man to reason this way: “Though my comfort is gone, yet the God of my comfort abides; though my joy is lost, yet the seeds of grace remain.” The best men’s joys are like glass, bright and brittle, and always in danger of breaking.

The second remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that the precious things that you still enjoy are far better than the joys and comforts that you have lost. Your union with Christ, your communion with Christ, your sonship, your saintship, your heirship, which you still enjoy through Christ, are far better than the comforts you have lost through sin. Even if your comforts are gone, yet your union and communion with Christ remain. Though your comforts are gone, yet you are a son, though#footnote[Jer 31:18, 19, 20.] a comfortless son; an heir, though a comfortless heir; a saint, though a comfortless saint. Though the bag of silver (your comforts) is lost, yet the#footnote[When someone reproached Faninus for his cheerfulness, set against Christ’s agony and sadness, he answered, “Christ was sad so that I might be merry; he had my sins, and I have his righteousness.”] box of jewels (your union with Christ, your communion with Christ, your sonship, your saintship, your heirship), which you still enjoy, is far better than the bag of silver you have lost. Indeed, the least of those precious jewels is worth more than all the comforts in the world. Well, let this be a heart-medicine to comfort you, a star to lead you, and a staff to support you: your box of jewels is safe, though your bag of silver is lost.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that your condition is no other than what has been the condition of those precious#footnote[Ps 51:12. Ps 30:6, 7. Job 23:6, 8, 9; 30:31. Lam 1:16. Matt 27:46.] souls whose names were written on the heart of Christ, and who are now at rest in the bosom of Christ. One day you will find them praising and rejoicing, the next day mourning and weeping. One day you will find them singing,#footnote[Ps 42:5.] “The #smallcaps[Lord] is our portion”; the next day sighing and arguing with themselves,#footnote[Lam 5:15.] “Why are you#super[+] downcast, O our souls? Why is our harp turned to mourning, and our flute to the sound of weeping?” etc.

The fourth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that the causes of joy and comfort are not always the same. Perhaps your former joy and comfort sprang from#footnote[The Spirit does not make a feast in the soul every day; he does not make every day a day of wearing the wedding robes.] the witness of the Spirit, he bearing witness to your soul that your nature was changed, your sins pardoned, your soul reconciled, #emph[etc.] Now the Spirit may, on some special occasion, bear witness to the soul that the heart of God is dearly set on him, that God loves him with an everlasting love, #emph[etc.], and yet the soul may never enjoy such a testimony again all the days of his life. Though the Spirit is a witnessing Spirit, it is not his office to witness every day to believers their share in God, Christ, heaven, #emph[etc.]

Or perhaps your former joy and comfort sprang from the newness and suddenness of the change in your condition. For a man in one hour to have#footnote[A pardon given unexpectedly into the hand of a criminal, when he is on the last step of the ladder, ready to be hanged, will cause much joy and rejoicing; the newness and suddenness of the change in his condition will cause his heart to leap and rejoice. Yet in the course of time much of his joy will abate, though his life is as dear to him still as ever it was.] his night turned into day, his darkness into light, his bitter into sweet, God’s frowns into smiles, his hatred into love, his hell into a heaven, must greatly gladden and comfort him. It cannot but make the heart leap and dance in him who in one hour sees Satan accusing him, his own heart condemning him, the eternal God frowning on him, the gates of heaven barred against him, all the creation standing armed at the least nod of God to execute vengeance on him, and the mouth of the pit of hell open to receive him. Now in this hour, for Christ to come to the stunned soul and say to it, “I have trodden the winepress of my Father’s wrath for you; I have laid down my life as a ransom for you; by my blood I have satisfied my Father’s justice, and pacified his anger, and procured his love for you; by my blood I have purchased the pardon of your sins, your freedom from hell, and your right to heaven,” #emph[etc.] Oh, how wonderfully this will cause the soul to leap for joy!

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider that God will restore and make up the comforts of his people. Though your candle is put out,#footnote[Hudson the martyr, deserted at the stake, went out from under his chain, and having prayed earnestly, was comforted immediately and suffered valiantly. So Mr. Glover, when he was within sight of the stake, cried out to his friend, “He has come, he has come,” meaning the Comforter whom Christ promised to send.] yet God will light it again and make it burn brighter than ever. Though your sun is clouded for the present, yet he who rides on the clouds will scatter those clouds, and cause the sun to shine and warm your heart as in former days. As the psalmist says,#footnote[Ps 71:20, 21.] “Though You have shown me many troubles and misfortunes, You will revive me once again. Even from the depths of the earth You will bring me back up. You will increase my honor and comfort me on every side.” God takes away a little comfort so that he may make room in the soul for a greater degree of comfort. The prophet Isaiah sweetly shows this:#footnote[Isa 57:18.] “I have seen his ways, but I will heal him; I will guide him and restore comforts to him and his mourners.” Bear up sweetly, O precious soul! Your storm will end in a calm, and your dark night in a sunny day; your mourning will be turned into rejoicing, and the waters of consolation will be sweeter and higher in your soul than#footnote[See also Ps 126:6 and Ps 42:7, 8.] ever. The mercy is surely yours, but the time of giving it is the Lord’s. Wait just a little, and you will find the Lord comforting you on every side.
